Your Kubernetes Events now show the actual cause of the Ingress provisioning failure.

Root cause: GKE is trying to configure a backend called `kube-system-default-http-backend`, but the expected Network Endpoint Group (NEG) does not exist.

The key error is:

```
Error syncing to GCP: error running backend syncing routine:
googleapi: Error 404:
The resource
'projects/weather-project-prod-511603/zones/asia-south2-a/networkEndpointGroups/k8s1-c1bd0ffd-kube-system-default-http-backend-80-7739e04a'
was not found
```

Your three application services successfully attached their endpoints:

- `user-service` — 2 endpoints attached
- `product-service` — 2 endpoints attached
- `order-service` — 2 endpoints attached

So the error points to the default HTTP backend resource, not necessarily your application services.

## 1. Check whether the default HTTP backend exists

Run:

```
kubectl get svc default-http-backend -n kube-system -o yaml
kubectl get endpoints default-http-backend -n kube-system
kubectl get pods -n kube-system | grep http
```

Also inspect its NEG annotation:

```
kubectl get svc default-http-backend -n kube-system \
  -o jsonpath='{.metadata.annotations.cloud\.google\.com/neg}{"\n"}'
```

GKE normally provides a default backend that returns HTTP 404 for requests that don't match your Ingress rules.&#x20;

